29:Automatically generated LLM harnesses offer a rich design space, but their
30:source-code, execution, and outcome diversity must be measured separately.
31:On BIRD text-to-SQL, trace inspection distinguishes absent, untriggered,
32:and broken mechanisms, and free generation yields 8.6\,pp of observed
33:oracle headroom across 1{,}169 questions; a one-seed MATH-500 probe
34:shows 10.6\% outcome disagreement and 4.75\,pp headroom. We formalize
35:one-run oracle gains, stable ranking, stable complementarity,
36:pre-execution selectability, and budgeted utility as five separately
37:reported claim classes, checked by ordering-invariance,
38:dominance-versus-complementarity, cloned-repeat, and condition-mixing
39:controls with hash-bound challenge re-execution; the procedure also
40:corrects one of our earlier verification claims. On the archived
41:factorial study the combined screening intervention estimates
42:$\RevisionPrimaryDelta$\,pp (95\% CI $\RevisionPrimaryCI$), supporting
43:the measurement contribution rather than intervention superiority. A
44:fresh pre-registered MATH-500 collection supplies the decisive
45:equal-budget control: an eight-harness panel and nine same-code clone
46:slots, each run for three independent repeats. The panel shows stable
47:member ranking, but stable complementarity is not established and its
48:discovery-selected advantage is indistinguishable from same-code
49:repeated execution. The framework turns apparent diversity into
50:auditable evidence and identifies the controls required before any
51:deployment claim.
